@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# prima-materia — exactness of recitation as the condition of efficacy
#
# One correspondence and the protocol that goes with it. The correspondence is
# held here rather than in any of the three tradition files because it belongs
# to none of them, and it is entered at all because the three witnesses stand
# on different sides of the contact question the collection is short of.
#

pmc:ExactnessAsConditionOfEfficacy a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "The exactness of the recitation is set as the condition of its efficacy"@en, "Die Genauigkeit der Rezitation ist als Bedingung ihrer Wirksamkeit gesetzt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Three transmissions make the same claim about spoken ritual language: the effect is not obtained unless the words are rendered as they stand. Two of them also say what a lapse does. One holds that an error, and equally a hesitation, is fatal to the efficacy of the address. Another holds that an error in a song may be fatal to the ceremony and that in some cases a single syllable does irreparable harm. The third requires the publicly chanted parts to be letter perfect and, in the passages read, states no consequence."@en ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:MaoriExactRecitation, pmc:NavajoSongExactness, pmc:ZuniPrayerExactness, pmc:HawaiianChantExactness ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:NavajoCeremonial, pmt:ZuniRitual ;
    pm:independenceGround "Not a claim of priority in time. The two American corpora were fixed in eighteen ninety-seven and, from fieldwork of the nineteen-twenties, in nineteen thirty-two, both after the Polynesian witness was printed in eighteen eighty-two. What carries the independence is that no route of contact between the Pacific and the American Southwest is available for the period in which any of the three corpora were fixed, so agreement between them cannot be one statement counted twice. Two things this ground does not settle and does not hide: both American corpora were gathered by ethnographers working in a discipline to which the rule of exact recitation was already familiar, and neither passage says whether the rule was volunteered in the field or asked for. The second is the gathering question and it stands open." ;
    skos:note "What the node claims and what it does not. Independent attestation is asserted for the two North American transmissions with respect to the Polynesian one and with respect to everything else this collection holds; no route of contact between the Pacific and the American Southwest is available for the periods in which any of these corpora were fixed. It is not asserted between those two: they are neighbours in one region, and a route between Pueblo and Athabaskan ceremonialism is neither absent nor established here. The Māori witness is therefore the third leg and not a third independent one, because its own contact route is entered as disputed."@en ;
    skos:note "A defect in the vocabulary shows at this node and is recorded rather than worked around. pm:contactRoute is a property of a single tradition, but contact is a relation between two. The value pm:contactRouteAbsent as the registry uses it means that no route runs to what the graph already held at the time of registration; it cannot answer whether a route runs between two registered traditions that are later brought together in one correspondence, which is exactly the question a convergence asks. See prima-materia#101 for the vocabulary defect and prima-materia#102 for the registry entries it affects."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search did not produce a tradition that denies the claim. What it produced is of a different kind and belongs here: the recorded versions of the same Māori address differ from one another across the eighteen-forties to the early nineteen-hundreds, and which rendering was valid was disputed among Māori at the time. A doctrine of one correct wording and a transmission that visibly carries several are not a contradiction, but they are the point at which the claim becomes examinable instead of edifying. The place that says so has not been read at the wording and stands at prima-materia#110."@en ;
    skos:note "Three witnesses agreeing on the form of a rule is a weaker finding than it looks unless the alternative is stated. The alternative exists and is common: traditions in which the sense of a prayer carries it and the wording may be varied, and traditions in which improvisation is the mark of the competent practitioner. That the three named here all fall on the fixed side is what the correspondence asserts. Where the rule holds and where it is absent has not been surveyed; the spheres left unsearched stand at prima-materia#116, the Indic parallel at prima-materia#109."@en ;
    skos:note "A fourth witness was added on 2026-09-04 and it is expressly not a fourth independent one. The Hawaiian transmission holds that a breath taken before the close of a phrase, a mistake, or even hesitation in pronouncing a word is a sign of ill-luck. It stands beside the Maori witness and not beyond it: both descend from one East Polynesian dispersal, dated archaeologically to the thirteenth century, so for the independence question the two Polynesian legs count as one. The count of transmissions carrying the rule goes from three to four; the count of independent ones does not move. Holding both numbers is what SPEC section 14 asks for, and this is the case that shows why one number alone would mislead."@en ;
    skos:note "What the fourth witness does add is a different thing, and it sharpens the correspondence rather than padding it. First, it names who suffers: the ill-luck falls on the person or family the chant honours and not on the reciter who made the fault, so the party that could report the lapse and the party that bears it are different people. Second, it names a countermeasure the transmission itself takes: a master of song gathers two or more of his fellows to edit and memorise the lines, and genealogies must be memorised by more than one reciter. A transmission that builds redundancy against its own stated failure condition has treated the condition as a real risk, which is evidence about the practice that a bare threat is not. Third, the same tradition sets the opposite rule for a different kind of utterance in the same file: at the consecration of the hula altar what must not lapse is the continuity of the voice, and the teacher is expressly free to change the prayer to avoid a silence. The exactness rule is therefore not a general doctrine about ritual speech even where it holds, and the correspondence should not be read as one."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Edward Shortland, Maori Religion and Mythology (London: Longmans, Green, 1882), p. 11", "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), sections 41-42, pp. 24-25", "Ruth L. Bunzel, Zuni Ritual Poetry, in Forty-Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of American Ethnology 1929-1930 (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1932), pp. 615-616", "Martha Warren Beckwith, The Kumulipo: A Hawaiian Creation Chant (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1951), chapter 7, pp. 35-36" .

pmc:RecitationExactnessExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Protocol on the exactness rule"@en, "Prüfprotokoll zur Genauigkeitsregel"@de ;
    skos:definition "An attempt to fail the claim that a misrecited ritual utterance does not obtain its effect. The claim is unusually well placed for this, because two of the three traditions name an outward event that follows a lapse, and one of them names who notices it and when."@en ;
    skos:note "The Navajo witness supplies the sharpest form of the test and it needs no laboratory: at the public ceremony many present know the songs, an error is caught, and the ceremony is broken off as fruitless. The condition can therefore be failed by the tradition's own reporting rather than by an outside measurement, which is what the falsifier below states."@en ;
    skos:note "The examination is entered at procedure without cases and not at no procedure devised, and the difference is the whole point of the scale. A condition has been stated here; nobody has yet collected the cases against it."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:ExactnessAsConditionOfEfficacy ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded case, from within one of these transmissions, of a ritual utterance known to have been misrecited and nonetheless reported by that transmission as having obtained its effect; or, on the Navajo side, a recorded public ceremony in which a detected error did not break off the proceeding." ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol drawn from the wording of the three witnesses; no cases collected" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:source "Washington Matthews, Navaho Legends, Memoirs of the American Folk-Lore Society V (Boston and New York: Houghton, Mifflin, 1897), section 42, pp. 24-25" .
